WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.899
What a working engineer takes from this.

2
00:00:02.179 --> 00:00:08.237
You’ll rarely hand-tune initializations in applied work; frameworks and pretrained checkpoints handle it.

3
00:00:08.517 --> 00:00:11.099
What transfers is the diagnostic stance.

4
00:00:11.379 --> 00:00:19.336
When a fine-tuning run diverges, plateaus, or produces NaNs, the questions from this lecture are the ones to ask, in order.

5
00:00:19.616 --> 00:00:21.728
Was the initial loss what you’d expect?

6
00:00:22.008 --> 00:00:23.928
Are any layers saturated or dead?

7
00:00:24.208 --> 00:00:28.560
Are gradients vanishing or exploding (gradient-norm logging catches this)?

8
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:34.557
Is the update size reasonable relative to the weights, or is the learning rate wrong for this model?

9
00:00:34.837 --> 00:00:37.803
That last question connects to model selection, too.

10
00:00:38.083 --> 00:00:49.325
Learning rates and schedules that work for one model size don’t transfer automatically to another, so when you compare two fine-tuned models, make sure each had a fair hyperparameter search.

11
00:00:49.605 --> 00:00:57.200
Otherwise you’re comparing tuning effort, not models, which is the same fairness rule Studio 3 applied to comparing prompts.
